.. thumbnail-parent-div-open

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="This example uses Wilson&#x27;s GF-matrix method to calculate normal modes for a linear triatomic (CO2) and a bent one (H2O) with TriatomicNormalModes. The molecule&#x27;s geometry and masses give the kinetic-energy matrix G=BM^{-1}B^T, where B is the Wilson B-matrix. A diagonal valence force field gives the potential-energy matrix F. Solving the eigenvalue problem GFL=L\Lambda, with \lambda_k=(2\pi c\tilde\nu_k)^2, gives the normal-mode wavenumbers. Changing only the bend entry of F shifts the bend frequency, while the stretches barely move.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/spectro/vibrational/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_triatomic_normal_modes_thumb.png
    :alt:

